+{"schemaVersion":"1","kind":"finding","id":"fnd_01M2HRGFABHFN9JE2TDMJX32RE","recordedAt":"2026-09-15T23:48:58Z","companyId":"example-co","title":"export tool writes query results (pii, financial) to plaintext EJSON files with no encryption or access controls","description":"src/common/exportsManager.ts:198-249: ExportsManager.startExport does fs.mkdir(this.exportsDirectoryPath, { recursive: true }) then createWriteStream(inProgressExport.exportPath) - snippet: const outputStream = createWriteStream(inProgressExport.exportPath); const ejsonTransform = this.docToEJSONStream(...); await pipeline([input.stream(), ejsonTransform, outputStream]) - with no encryption, no file-mode restriction and no vault or KMS reference. The default folder is the user home .mongodb/mongodb-mcp/exports directory (src/common/config/configUtils.ts:53-55) and each session writes under a per-session subfolder (lines 363-372). Files carry full find/aggregate documents from the client-data and order stores that env/prod.json classifies as pii and financial, are served back by resource URI and, when the server runs locally, by raw filesystem path (src/tools/mongodb/read/export.ts:119-124). Retention is a partial compensating control: exports expire after exportTimeoutMs (default 300000 ms, request overrides can only lower it) with a cleanup cycle (lines 297-338) and the folder is deleted recursively on shutdown (lines 110-126), but during their life the data sits unencrypted on the host. Severity is the catalog defaultSeverity of sebi-cscrf-2024:PR.DS.S1. DPDP Rule 6(1)(a) is a readiness mapping. SLA topic: encryption. Commit aaa72a040db4c32f3d6488d5e28e2892a68e9ee0.\n\nRefutation: evidence lens not refuted (Every evidence check passed on bytes I opened myself: the SARIF export at the cited path contains a SCH-PII-02 result at index 5 (confirmed by ruleId-chain probes: 5 ruleIds before it match, 6 do not) located at src/common/exportsManager.ts with startLine 198 and endLine 249, carrying the candidate's fingerprint value and the verbatim message phrase 'client-data and order stores', and its rule declares defaultSeverity high with severityBasis sebi-cscrf-2024:PR.DS.S1; the checkout at pinned commit aaa72a040db4c32f3d6488d5e28e2892a68e9ee0 exhibits the gap exactly as described (createWriteStream with no mode option, no encryption or KMS/vault reference anywhere in the write path, exportsPath default ~/.mongodb/mongodb-mcp/exports at configUtils.ts:53-55, per-session subfolder at 363-372, resource-URI and raw-path serving at export.ts:119-124, exportTimeoutMs default 300_000 with onlyLowerThanBaseValueOverride at userConfig.ts:194-198, cleanup at 297-338 and recursive shutdown deletion at 110-126), with no overriding compensating control in the repo (chmod/umask appear only in the src/ui frontend bundle and 'encrypt' only in atlas openapi.d.ts type declarations, neither in the export path), the condition is reachable in the deployed prod environment (env/prod.json dataClassification pii+financial, partner exposure) and independently corroborated by ledger observation obs_01M2H1VF3N8NATYT9XVASD12XF, severity high equals the catalog defaultSeverity of PR.DS.S1 (sebi-cscrf-2024.catalog.json line 3222, whose guidance prescribes high for unencrypted stores on the encryption topic) so no lower severity is supported, all cited controls exist and apply (RBI 140 is a Cryptographic Controls clause whose guidance names probe-schemas; dpdp-rules-2025:6(1)(a) is a PR.DS.S1 catalog mapping; NIST SC-28 likewise), and the fingerprint is unused elsewhere in the ledger; the only unverifiable item is the export's sha256 digest (no independent record in the workspace to compare against), and evidence[1] is a byte-identical duplicate of evidence[0] minus its description.); regulatory-mapping lens not refuted (Every cited instrument binds example-co (sebi-cscrf-2024 via active mid-size-re stock-broker/DP registrations, rbi-cyber-tech-directions-2026 via the active nbfc-middle-layer registration and in force since 2026-07-31, dpdp-rules-2025 and the global nist-800-53-r5), each controlId exists in its catalog with prose covering unencrypted pii/financial data at rest — the PR.DS.S1 guidance prescribes exactly this finding ('Each unencrypted store ... is a high finding citing sebi-cscrf-2024:PR.DS.S1, topic encryption'), RBI 140's guidance names probe-schemas checking that sensitive fields are encrypted/tokenised, and 6(1)(a) mandates encryption of personal data — the most specific Indian instrument (SEBI, the company's primary licence and the ledger's existing SCH-PII-02 control key) is first with the global crosswalk last, and severity high equals the catalog defaultSeverity of PR.DS.S1 verified at sebi-cscrf-2024.catalog.json line 3222 per maxwell-conventions section 4 (the finding carries no CVSS score and the tool's rule properties use defaultSeverity/severityBasis, not security-severity), with the 'readiness mapping' label on Rule 6(1)(a) matching the DPDP catalog's own commencement rule (rule 6 controls commence 2027-05-13; gaps before then are raised at catalog severity as readiness gaps) and 'SLA topic: encryption' being a valid vocab topic that the SEBI guidance says falls to the sla-table defaults. )","severity":"high","confidence":"confirmed","controlIds":["sebi-cscrf-2024:PR.DS.S1","rbi-cyber-tech-directions-2026:140","dpdp-rules-2025:6(1)(a)"],"regulatoryRefs":[{"regulator":"SEBI","instrument":"sebi-cscrf-2024","controlId":"PR.DS.S1"},{"regulator":"RBI","instrument":"rbi-cyber-tech-directions-2026","controlId":"140"},{"regulator":"MeitY","instrument":"dpdp-rules-2025","controlId":"6(1)(a)"},{"regulator":"NIST","instrument":"nist-800-53-r5","controlId":"SC-28"}],"target":{"type":"repo","appId":"mcp-gateway","repoId":"mongodb-mcp-server"},"location":{"path":"src/common/exportsManager.ts","startLine":198,"endLine":249},"fingerprint":"aaa072c5d88f48f0cfbdb1873433912d61e49104982c457024c9e0ee45025b2f","source":{"kind":"sarif","rule