+{"schemaVersion":"1","kind":"finding","id":"fnd_01M2K7B0MJ3239WAE0AKHRMK5E","recordedAt":"2026-09-15T23:48:58Z","companyId":"example-co","title":"Repo ships developer-harness configuration for GitHub Copilot outside harnessesAllowed","description":".github/copilot-instructions.md:3-4 configures the GitHub Copilot coding agent for this repository; .github/agents/agentic-workflows.agent.md defines a GitHub Agentic Workflows and Copilot Cloud dispatcher agent (line 191: bash tools are enabled by default without restriction), with .github/workflows/copilot-setup-steps.yml and .vscode/ alongside. None of these harnesses is in aiCodingPolicy.harnessesAllowed (claude-code, opencode), so ExampleCo engineers using the stock configuration run an unapproved agent harness on the mcp-gateway codebase, and the setup wizard (src/setup/aiTool.ts) actively configures four further unapproved harnesses (Cursor, VS Code/Copilot, Windsurf, Claude Desktop). This is upstream developer-harness configuration audited against the SDLC policy, not a deployed customer-facing agent. Severity is the catalog defaultSeverity of sebi-cscrf-2024 GV.PO.S1. SLA topic: patch-sla. Refutation: evidence lens not refuted (Every cited fact checks out in files I opened: .github/copilot-instructions.md exists as the repo's GitHub Copilot coding-agent instruction file, .github/agents/agentic-workflows.agent.md:191 states 'Bash tools are enabled by default - Don't restrict bash commands unnecessarily', .github/workflows/copilot-setup-steps.yml and .vscode/ (empty settings.json, vitest-only extensions.json, so no override) ship alongside, src/setup/aiTool.ts registers exactly the four further harnesses Cursor/VS Code (Copilot)/Windsurf/Claude Desktop beyond claudeCode/opencode, policy.json aiCodingPolicy.harnessesAllowed is exactly [claude-code, opencode] with no per-repo exception mechanism and no 'harnessesAllowed' override anywhere in the checkout, the SARIF export exists and contains a result with ruleId AGENT-POL-01 on .github/copilot-instructions.md with startLine 1 endLine 5 and a message citing harnessesAllowed whose rule carries defaultSeverity high, and severity high equals the catalog defaultSeverity of sebi-cscrf-2024 GV.PO.S1 (sebi-cscrf-2024.catalog.json line 789) - a developer-harness SDLC finding whose reachability is the repo itself (pinned commit aaa72a04 matches the context), so deployed-environment reachability does not apply and no lower severity is supported.); regulatory-mapping lens not refuted (Every check passes: sebi-cscrf-2024 is in frameworksInScope and applies to example-co (active mid-size-re stock-broker/DP registrations, IN jurisdiction, not repealed, effective 2025-08-31) and is cited first; GV.PO.S1 exists in the SEBI catalog with defaultSeverity \"high\" - exactly the severity the candidate derives - and its policy prose plus the governing agent-graph-auditor checklist (AGENT-POL-01 row) prescribe this control for a harness outside aiCodingPolicy.harnessesAllowed, which I verified is [claude-code, opencode] in sdlc/policy.json; owasp-agentic-top10-2026 ASI04 is a valid registry id (agentic supply chain, \"Inventory and verify provenance of agent frameworks...\") matching the owasp-agentic-top10-2026:asi04 tag and the unvetted-harness weakness, and nist-ssdf-800-218 PO.1.1 is a valid SSDF task id that the same checklist names as this rule's extra ref; the only caveat I could not clear is the gitignored single-line SARIF export beyond its first ~2000 chars (result 8 and the AGENT-POL-01 rule entry unopened, so a hypothetical properties.security-severity override is unconfirmed, though the emission spec prescribes properties.defaultSeverity only and the export head confirms that shape), and note the auditor row's conditional Other-Indian ref rbi-it-outsourcing-md-2023:8 is repealed since 28 Nov 2025 (successor rbi-outsourcing-risk-directions-2025), which the candidate correctly omits rather than cites.).","severity":"high","confidence":"con