+{"schemaVersion":"1","kind":"finding","id":"fnd_01M2KM42DZE09A2PZ557A7SX2F","recordedAt":"2026-09-15T23:48:58Z","companyId":"example-co","title":".gitignore ignores only the exact name .env, leaving .env* variants and key files trackable in a public repo","description":".gitignore line 8 contains only `.env`, so .env.local, .env.development, .env.staging and other .env* variants are not ignored, nor are *.pem, *.key, kubeconfig or dump extensions. The repo's own .vscode/launch.json loads ${workspaceFolder}/.env for its Debug Tests and Debug generate-release-notes configurations, and the server takes credentials through MDB_MCP_CONNECTION_STRING, so developers will hold env files with live connection strings; the repo visibility is public, so a stray `git add` publishes them. The tracked tests/integration/fixtures/server.key and server.pem are deliberate self-signed test fixtures and would need explicit negation lines after widening the patterns. Severity is the catalog defaultSeverity of sebi-cscrf-2024 PR.DS.S4 (high).\n\nImpact: Live MongoDB connection strings or API keys in .env.local or stray *.pem/*.key files would be committed by default in a public repository; the company gitleaks CI gate (blocking at low) would catch only recognisable patterns after the fact and the local hook scans AWS patterns only, so the PR.DS.S4 data-leak-prevention control is weakened at the developer layer.\n\nRemediation: Add `.env*` (with `!.env.example`), `*.pem`, `*.key` (with negations for the tracked test fixtures), `kubeconfig*` and dump extensions (*.sql, *.dump, *.sqlite*) to .gitignore, and add a pre-commit gitleaks run alongside git-secrets.","severity":"high","confidence":"confirmed","controlIds":["sebi-cscrf-2024:PR.DS.S4"],"regulatoryRefs":[{"regulator":"SEBI","instrument":"sebi-cscrf-2024","controlId":"PR.DS.S4"},{"regulator":"NIST","instrument":"nist-ssdf-800-218","controlId":"PO.5.2"}],"target":{"type":"repo","appId":"mcp-gateway","repoId":"mongodb-mcp-server"},"loca