+{"schemaVersion":"1","kind":"finding","id":"fnd_01M2KM42DZQK8J4EZEB68ZQ103","recordedAt":"2026-09-16T02:18:09Z","companyId":"example-co","title":"Integration-test mongod published unauthenticated on all interfaces of developer laptops","description":"tests/integration/tools/mongodb/mongot-community-setup/docker-compose.yml publishes mongod as \"0:27017\" (line 12), which binds a random host port on 0.0.0.0 rather than 127.0.0.1; config/mongod.conf sets bindIp: 0.0.0.0 (line 7); and the compose command (lines 4-7) starts mongod without --auth or a keyFile, so the test replica set accepts unauthenticated connections even though init-mongo.sh creates a scoped mongotUser. The dev-tier environment record (applications/mcp-gateway/env/dev.json) describes the dev host fleet as developer-laptops reached over the docker socket, so this database is the one port a developer machine exposes. Mitigating context: containers are ephemeral test fixtures holding sample data, and the port is random rather than the well-known 27017. Severity is the catalog defaultSeverity of sebi-cscrf-2024 PR.IP.S1 (high).\n\nImpact: Any network a developer laptop joins (home or public wifi, co-working networks) can reach an unauthenticated MongoDB instance on a published port, giving a foothold into the dev tier that also holds the docker-socket credential; SEBI CSCRF PR.IP.S1 requires port whitelisting and hardened baselines, PR.AA.S2 requires network segmentation.\n\nRemediation: Bind the published port to loopback (127.0.0.1:27017:27017), start mongod with --auth plus a keyFile for the replica set, and keep bindIp scoped to the compose network.","severity":"high","confidence":"confirmed","controlIds":["sebi-cscrf-2024:PR.IP.S1","sebi-cscrf-2024:PR.AA.S2"],"regulatoryRefs":[{"regulator":"SEBI","instrument":"sebi-cscrf-2024","controlId":"PR.IP.S1"},{"regulator":"SEBI","instrument":"sebi-cscrf-2024","controlId":"PR.AA.S2"},{"regulator":"CIS","instrument":"cis-controls-8.1","controlId":"4.1"}],"targ